@ArchieAshford  Don't need to watch it again, it's the consumers fault for not properly researching the product if they try to pair this with a subpar CPU, say what you would like but the 5500 is ranked 189th and the 5600 is ranked 141st out of available CPUs, neither even cracks the top 100. So yes, they are dated CPUs considering all the other options available. 

Just because something is reasonably priced, doesn't mean it is meant to be pair with subpar equipment. Would be like expecting a V8 to work well in a ford escort. Intel released a CPU list, this is no different than when the A series came out and everyone cried it didn't work with their old motherboards that didn't have rebar. 

If people can't afford an extra 100 bucks for a better CPU when building a new rig, or they don't have the money for the B580 and an upgraded CPU on their platform, then they have bigger issues. Get a different GPU if you don't have the hardware to properly run it, pretty simple.

@ArchieAshford  listen, repeating the same thing over and over again doesn't make you right. The only thing you are right about is this being a budget entry level card, but your mistake is thinking it is an entry level card for a "budget build"

This is not a card for an old budget build... This is a card on NEW architecture that is meant to be paired with THE NEWEST HARDWARE. You don't come out with new tech for the purpose of making it "dated". The new cards are meant to be future proof to work on the newest hardware, and tech that is yet to come out. It's not a NEW card meant to be catered to OLD hardware. I don't know why this is a hard concept for people to grasp.

Should we stop making CPUs that need new sockets and new motherboards? Should we just stay on AM4 and AM5 forever? It's the same principle just with a GPU, it is required to have newer hardware.

@ArchieAshford  I haven't been proven wrong on anything. Intel says it requires newer hardware and lists the recommended parts. It is an entry level card for a new system on new hardware. 

Just because you want it to be the cheap option for old hardware doesn't mean it is, and doesn't mean it was made for that. Get a 7600 for 260 bucks if you need a series of card that will perform well on your platform. 

Get the B580 for a build on AM5 or LGA 1700, its really not a hard concept.

